readingTasks.push(
['Urban gardens','Community gardens transform unused urban land into shared growing spaces. Besides providing fresh produce, they create opportunities for neighbors to exchange knowledge. Their long-term success often depends on secure access to land and clear responsibilities for maintenance.','Which factor supports long-term success?','Secure access to land and clear responsibilities.','Replacing all public parks.','Growing only one crop.'],
['Digital preservation','Digitizing a historical document makes it easier to distribute, but a digital copy is not automatically permanent. File formats may become obsolete and storage devices can fail. Archives therefore maintain multiple copies and periodically migrate files to supported formats.','Why do archives migrate files?','To maintain accessibility as technology changes.','To make documents historically older.','To eliminate the need for backups.'],
['Active learning','In active learning, students engage with material by solving problems, explaining concepts or evaluating evidence. These activities can reveal misunderstandings that remain hidden during passive listening. Their effectiveness depends on meaningful tasks and appropriate feedback.','What advantage does active learning offer?','It can reveal misunderstandings.','It guarantees identical results for all learners.','It eliminates the need for feedback.'],
['Rain gardens','Rain gardens are shallow planted areas designed to collect runoff from roofs and paved surfaces. Water temporarily accumulates before infiltrating the soil. Unlike conventional drains, rain gardens can reduce runoff while providing habitat, although their design must suit local soil conditions.','How do rain gardens differ from conventional drains?','They encourage infiltration and can provide habitat.','They prevent every flood.','They require no consideration of soil.'],
['Open standards','Open technical standards allow different manufacturers to develop compatible products. This can reduce dependence on a single supplier and encourage competition. However, a published standard alone does not ensure interoperability if implementations interpret its requirements differently.','What limitation does the passage identify?','Different implementations may interpret requirements differently.','Open standards prohibit competition.','Compatible products require one manufacturer.'],
['Museum lighting','Museums must balance visibility with the preservation of sensitive objects. Light exposure can gradually damage paper, textiles and pigments. Curators may therefore use low illumination, limit exhibition duration or rotate objects to reduce cumulative exposure.','Why might museums rotate objects?','To limit cumulative light exposure.','To increase the temperature of galleries.','To avoid documenting collections.'],
['Urban freight','Delivering goods to city centers creates traffic and demand for loading space. Consolidation centers allow shipments from several suppliers to be combined before the final delivery. This can reduce vehicle trips, but additional handling may increase costs.','What trade-off is described?','Fewer vehicle trips versus additional handling costs.','More suppliers versus less available goods.','Higher speeds versus shorter roads.'],
['Scientific uncertainty','A scientific estimate often includes uncertainty because measurements and models have limitations. Reporting uncertainty does not imply that a finding is useless. Instead, it helps readers judge how precisely the evidence supports a conclusion and whether further measurements are worthwhile.','What is the purpose of reporting uncertainty?','To help readers assess the precision of evidence.','To demonstrate that every conclusion is false.','To discourage further measurement.'],
['Reusable packaging','Reusable containers may reduce waste when they complete enough use cycles to offset the resources required for their production and cleaning. Return rates, transport distances and washing methods all influence the environmental outcome. Reuse is therefore a system rather than simply a material choice.','What is the main idea?','The benefits of reuse depend on the entire system.','Reusable containers always have lower impacts.','Washing has no environmental cost.'],
['Peer instruction','During peer instruction, students first answer a conceptual question individually and then discuss their reasoning with classmates. They answer again after discussion. The process encourages students to articulate assumptions and evaluate alternative explanations rather than merely memorize a correct option.','Why do students discuss their answers?','To examine reasoning and alternative explanations.','To avoid answering independently at any stage.','To replace all conceptual questions with lectures.'],
['Mountain vegetation','Temperature generally decreases with elevation, influencing which plants can survive on mountains. However, slope orientation also affects sunlight and moisture. Consequently, vegetation at the same elevation may differ substantially between opposite sides of a mountain.','Why can vegetation differ at the same elevation?','Slope orientation affects sunlight and moisture.','Elevation has no effect on plants.','All mountain soils are identical.'],
['Public libraries','Modern public libraries provide access to books, digital resources and shared learning spaces. Some also offer workshops that help residents develop practical skills. Evaluating a library solely by the number of books borrowed may overlook these broader community functions.','What does the author suggest about borrowing statistics?','They may overlook other library functions.','They measure every community benefit.','They should never be collected.'],
['Modular construction','Modular construction involves manufacturing building components away from the final site. Controlled factory conditions can improve consistency and reduce weather-related delays. Nevertheless, transport restrictions and the need for early design decisions may limit its suitability for some projects.','Which limitation is mentioned?','Transport restrictions and early design decisions.','Factory production always reduces consistency.','Modules cannot be manufactured indoors.'],
['Language exposure','Regular exposure to a foreign language helps learners recognize common patterns. Yet exposure alone may not resolve persistent grammatical errors. Targeted practice and feedback can draw attention to distinctions that learners otherwise overlook while concentrating on meaning.','Why is targeted practice useful?','It highlights distinctions learners may overlook.','It makes exposure unnecessary.','It prevents learners from understanding meaning.'],
['Citizen participation','Public consultations can help planners understand local needs before implementing a policy. However, participants may not represent all affected residents. Combining open meetings with targeted outreach can reduce the risk that less visible groups are excluded.','Why combine consultation methods?','To include groups that might otherwise be overlooked.','To guarantee unanimous agreement.','To avoid collecting residents’ opinions.']
);